---
/**
 * The site's one loading indicator for "this region is fetching something".
 *
 * A 2px track carrying `.progress-sweep` (utilities/utils.css) — the same bar the seller dashboard
 * shows while a panel hydrates, extracted here the second time it was needed rather than copied.
 *
 * **It replaced dimming the region, and the reason is worth keeping.** The first attempt at a
 * loading cue for a filter faded the results to 35% opacity while the next set arrived. That says
 * "loading" only by taking something away: the content a shopper is reading goes grey, the page
 * looks broken rather than busy, and on a fast connection it is a flash of exactly that. A bar adds
 * a signal instead of degrading the content — and it is what this project already uses, so it needs
 * no new visual vocabulary (owner, 2026-08-03).
 *
 * **It only appears if the wait is real.** The sweep is wrapped in a span whose fade-in is delayed
 * 250ms, so a request that resolves sooner never shows anything at all: the element is inserted and
 * removed before its own animation begins. The delay lives in CSS rather than a JS timer because
 * that is one less thing to cancel, and because a superseded request simply clears the track.
 *
 * The track itself stays in the DOM at all times — removing it would shift everything below by 2px
 * the moment loading ended, which is its own small jump.
 *
 * **Why the track is masked at both ends (owner, 2026-08-03).** The sweep is a 50%-wide element
 * whose gradient fades to transparent at each end, travelling from -200% to +200% inside an
 * `overflow-hidden` track. That reads soft only while it is fully inside. At the start and end of
 * every cycle it is half outside, and what you see is the gradient sliced through its solid middle
 * and pinned flat against the track's boundary — a hard vertical stop that made the bar look
 * chopped by the container's side padding rather than travelling through it. The mask fades the
 * outer 7% of the track to transparent, so the sweep thins out as it leaves instead of hitting a
 * wall: narrow at the edges, full-bodied through the middle, which is the shape it always claimed
 * to have. Chosen over widening the track to full-bleed because the bar describes the container's
 * content and should not out-measure it.
 *
 * Drive it from script with `startLoadingSweep(el)` / `stopLoadingSweep(el)` (lib/loading-sweep.ts).
 */
interface Props {
  /** DOM id, so the page's own script can find the track. */
  id: string;
  /** Announced to a screen reader — dimming announces nothing at all. */
  label: string;
  class?: string;
}
const { id, label, class: className = '' } = Astro.props;
---

<div
  id={id}
  class:list={[
    'h-[2px] overflow-hidden',
    // Symmetric, so it needs no [dir=rtl] variant — the sweep travels the same
    // way in both directions and dissolves into either end.
    '[mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,transparent_0%,#000_7%,#000_93%,transparent_100%)]',
    '[-webkit-mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,transparent_0%,#000_7%,#000_93%,transparent_100%)]',
    className,
  ]}
  role="status"
  aria-label={label}
></div>
